  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potent storm system moves across PA tonight and\nFri with a severe threat Fri aftn/eve. \n\nA surface low moving through the Midwest will cross the Great\nLakes tonight and Fri. A warm front will bring widespread \nrain/showers overnight, with some elevated thunder possible (20\npct chc). A break with some sunshine during the morning and \nearly afternoon will destabilize the atmos over much of the CWA \nFri. The break may not be an hour over our NW few counties, but \nthe SE will have 6-9hrs to recover from the overnight rain. This\ncould allow storms to form - mainly right along the front as it\npasses through. But, some could pop before it arrives in any \nlocation. The bulk shear will get between 40-60KT and CAPE \nbetween 500-1000 J/kg over the SE third to half of the CWA, \nhighest in the Lower Susq. SPC mentions a 2% tornado risk over \nthe Susq valley as some positive EHI shows up and LCLs may stay \nbelow 3kft there. It is still 2026, after all. The frontal \ntiming is roughly 15Z in Warren Co, and 00Z in Lancaster Co. \nAdded enhanced TS wording to the grids and mentions of severe \nand poss tornadoes to the HWO.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Windy with first flakes possible on the higher \nterrain of the north Fri night/Sat morning\n\nPost-frontal gradient is pretty tight with 10mb of drop across\nthe state overnight and Sat morning. 50+KT winds behind the \nfront look like they will stay AOA 6-7kft. This should be tough\nto mix down, esp without the wind dir in the llvls being more \nvertically-homogenous than currently expected. While we have\ncontinued to mention some 35-40MPH gusts, the risk for advy\nlevel (40+KT) gusts looks quite a bit lower than it has looked \nrecently.\n\nThe CAA will drop 8H temps 15-20C in less than a day. The initial\nsurge of cold will make a very short-fetch flow of sig lower \nair vs lake water temps which is 64F at ERI and 61F off Oswego,\nNY. Expect lake effect clouds later Fri night and very early \nSat AM. However, many models are not generating much meas precip\nthen. GFS sounding at BFD puts the inversion height at only \n5kft starting at 06Z and it stays there all night/morning, with \nthe NAM even more-shallow with the cold air coming across the \nlower lakes. Temps are favorable for SN up north. So, we still \nexpect any lingering SHRA to turn to SHSN overnight. But, the \nfetch turns quickly northerly and then the air becomes drier in\nthe sunrise to mid-morning time frame. A dusting is possible, \nbut unlikely in the northern mtns. \n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Cold weather may end the growing season for many\nplaces Sun morning. \n\nBig sfc high moves in for the weekend, supplying dry but cold\nweather for Sat and Sun. Sat will hold the coldest daytime \ntemps with temps getting just above 40F in the NE and 52F in the\nSE. Those maxes will be 22-28F lower than Friday's maxes. \n\nSat AM could be sub-freezing for the nrn tier. But, the clouds\nexpected may keep temps milder than the current NBM mean.\nMAV/MET numbers are slightly milder. So far, only McKean Co has\nbeen taken off the list of zones to need any further \nfrost/freeze products. But, we could add a county or two in the\nN to that list Sat AM.\n\nSunday morning will be the most-likely time for sub-freezing \ntemps across much of the CWA. However, it will be Sunday \nmorning's temps which could end the growing season for a \nsignificant portion of the CWA. A widespread frost is almost a \ncertainty as winds decrease and go mainly calm Sun AM. We'll be \nconsidering appropriate frost/freeze products, but it is still \ntoo early to be that confident on details. Pretty cold again Mon\nAM, but not quite as cold - and some of the area may not need \nany more frost/freeze products by then. High clouds from a \npossible coastal low could reach up this far north, and may \naffect the temps Mon AM.",
